@prefix tmk:     <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/> .
@prefix tmkc:    <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/concept/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf:     <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix prov:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/authority>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "TM-Knowledge — authority module"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """What kind of thing is speaking, and with what force.

This is the module CLAUDE.md rule 5 lives in. The Manual states the Registrar's
practice; it does not bind the Registrar's discretion and it is not legislation.
The distinction is carried here as a class disjointness rather than as a flag,
because a flag can be dropped by a projection and a disjointness cannot be
satisfied by an assertion that ignores it."""@en-AU ;
    owl:imports <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/document> ;
    owl:versionInfo "draft — not approved" .

# --- what can speak --------------------------------------------------------

tmk:Authority a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Authority"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Something that can be cited in support of a proposition. Legislation, a decision, or the Registrar's own practice material — all three, and the point of the module is that they are not interchangeable."@en-AU .

tmk:Legislation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Document ;
    rdfs:label "Legislation"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "An instrument. This corpus holds two: the Trade Marks Act 1995 and the Trade Marks Regulations 1995."@en-AU .

tmk:LegislativeProvision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Passage , tmk:Authority ;
    rdfs:label "Legislative provision"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A section, regulation, Schedule clause or container of an instrument, keyed by its provision ref. It is law."@en-AU .

tmk:LegislativeUnit a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:LegislativeProvision ;
    rdfs:label "Legislative unit"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A numbered unit inside a provision — subsection, paragraph, definition.

Modelled as a subclass rather than as a sibling because a ref does not say what
level it addresses: 228 legislation refs in the pinned corpus cannot be placed
by grammar alone (Q-18, ADR-0025). Anything true of a provision is true of a
unit of one, so the subclass licenses nothing the corpus cannot support."""@en-AU .

tmk:ManualInstruction a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Authority ;
    rdfs:label "Manual instruction"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A statement of the Registrar's practice, sourced from the Manual.

It is **not law**. It does not bind the Registrar's discretion. A system that
lets a Manual instruction be read as a statutory requirement has committed the
error PU-0004 names, and that error is a live one in this corpus: the Manual
quotes s 43 in full inside TMM/Part29/1#1, so an answer can appear to cite the
Act while having read only the Manual (CQ-0001's caveat)."""@en-AU .

tmk:Decision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Authority ;
    rdfs:label "Decision"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A decided matter the Manual cites. **Citation level only.** No decision text
exists anywhere in this programme (Q-11, HANDOFF Q6), so a decision node carries
a citation, party names where the Manual gives them, and the Manual's own
account of it — never the reasons. 'Which cases interpret this test' is
answerable; 'what did the court hold' is not, and PU-0005 is the output that
pretends otherwise.

Who decided is read off the case id's series by `ontology.decisions` and put in
one of the two subclasses below. A series that reports courts and registry
decisions alike (IPR, RPC, FSR, AIPC) leaves the decision here, unclassified,
rather than guessing (CLAUDE.md rule 6)."""@en-AU .

tmk:JudicialDecision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "Judicial decision"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A court's decision — the High Court, the Federal Court, a state Supreme Court, an English court. An exercise of judicial power; where the court sits in the hierarchy is tmk:decidedAt, as far as the citation says (review C7)."@en-AU .

tmk:AdministrativeDecision a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "Administrative decision"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A decision of the Registrar's delegate — a hearing officer's, reported as ATMO or in the Official Journal (AOJP) — or of the Commissioner of Patents' (APO).

The office's own earlier view, not a court's. Until 2026-10-08 the 108 ATMO
decisions the Manual cites were typed as judicial decisions beside the High
Court's, so an answer citing one presented the office's view on a court's
footing. The owner ruled them apart: "An administrative decision is different
to a judicial decision" (review C7, ADR-0121)."""@en-AU .

tmk:AdministrativeDecision owl:disjointWith tmk:JudicialDecision .

tmk:decisionKind a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "decision kind"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "administrative, judicial or unclassified — from the case id's series, by a lookup and nothing else."@en-AU .

tmk:jurisdiction a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "jurisdiction"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "AU or UK, where the series says. An English decision is persuasive at most; the graph has to be able to say which one it is."@en-AU .

tmk:decidedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "decided at"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The court or office that decided it, where the series fixes it — the High Court, the Full Federal Court, the Registrar's delegate. Absent where a report series covers several courts."@en-AU .

tmk:Guidance a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Authority ;
    rdfs:label "Guidance"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Non-binding material that is neither legislation nor a decision. Declared for the roadmap's model; nothing in the approved set is classified into it yet."@en-AU .

# --- the disjointness that must survive into retrieval output --------------

tmk:ManualInstruction owl:disjointWith tmk:LegislativeProvision .

tmk:ManualPassage a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "Manual passage"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """A passage of the Trade Marks Manual: a statement of the Registrar's practice, never law.

Asserted on every Manual chunk by the same structural rule that gives it
`tmk:authorityKind "practice"` — it came from the Manual — so the disjointness
below has something to guard on real data. Until 2026-10-08 the only class it
guarded was tmk:ManualInstruction, which is applied to entity mentions and to no
passage, so the guard fired only on test fixtures (review F1, ADR-0121).

A Manual passage may *quote* the Act — Part 29.1 quotes section 43 in full — and
is still a Manual passage: the words are the Manual's account of the law, and an
answer resting on it has read the Manual (CQ-0001's caveat, PU-0004)."""@en-AU .

tmk:ManualPassage owl:disjointWith tmk:LegislativeProvision .

# --- what an authority does ------------------------------------------------

tmk:authorityKind a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Authority ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "authority kind"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """`law` | `practice` | `decision`. Redundant with the class, on purpose: it is
what a retrieval projection carries when it has dropped the types, and it is
what a shape checks when it wants to catch a projection that dropped them
silently. A SPARQL result that names a passage without this is missing the one
fact CLAUDE.md rule 5 requires it to carry."""@en-AU .

tmk:statedIn a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "stated in"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The exact passage a proposition was read out of. Not 'about' — 'is written here'."@en-AU .

tmk:attributedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:LegalProposition ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Authority ;
    rdfs:label "attributed to"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """The authority a proposition is being *presented as coming from*.

Separate from tmk:statedIn, which is where the words are, and the separation is
the whole point. PU-0004 — "Section 43 of the Act requires the connotation to be
obvious, direct and immediate" — is a proposition stated in a Manual passage and
attributed to the Act. Every word of it is in the corpus and the attribution is
wrong, which is why it is worth a constraint rather than a test: a shape blocks
publication, a test only reports (shapes/README.md).

`shapes/authority.ttl` fails any proposition stated in a practice passage and
attributed to a legislative provision."""@en-AU .

tmk:hasCitation a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:label "has citation"@en-AU .

# --- the citation edge, kept as an object -----------------------------------

tmk:Citation a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Citation"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """One reference from a passage to an authority, reified so that upstream's
trust metadata survives (CLAUDE.md rule 3).

A bare triple `chunk -> provision` throws away the two things that separate an
author's assertion from a machine's inference, and once thrown away they cannot
be recovered from the graph. So the edge is a node: it carries how it was found
and how certain that was, and `tmk:citesProvision` exists beside it only as a
convenience for queries that genuinely do not care."""@en-AU .

tmk:citingPassage a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "citing passage"@en-AU .

tmk:citedAuthority a owl:ObjectProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:label "cited authority"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Deliberately without an `rdfs:range`. 17 refs cited from the in-scope chunks land on no provision this corpus holds (recon §4); a range of tmk:LegislativeProvision would classify those targets as provisions on the strength of being cited, which is exactly the inference the unresolved list exists to prevent."@en-AU .

tmk:extraction a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "extraction"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "`href` — the Manual's authors linked it themselves, the strongest provenance the corpus has. `regex` — upstream read it out of the prose. Upstream's word, never reinterpreted (CLAUDE.md rule 3)."@en-AU .

tmk:certainty a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "certainty"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment """`explicit` | `default` | `ambiguous`, present on `regex` edges only.

`default` is upstream's inference from a bare 'section 43' — an inference, not a
statement by IP Australia. `ambiguous` is upstream refusing to choose between
instruments, and it is never resolved here: an ambiguous edge is a reason to put
a passage in front of a person, never a reason to drop it (Q-07)."""@en-AU .

tmk:mention a owl:DatatypeProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "mention"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "The surface text upstream matched, where it recorded one."@en-AU .

tmk:citationResolves a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Citation ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "citation resolves"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the cited ref lands on something this corpus holds. False is a fact about the corpus, not a defect in the citation — s 114 of the 1905 Act is cited by Part 29.3.1 and is held nowhere in the programme (Q-24)."@en-AU .

tmk:citesProvision a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "cites provision"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "Convenience shortcut past the Citation node. Safe for 'which passages mention s 43'; wrong for anything that turns on how the edge was found, which is what tmk:Citation is for."@en-AU .

tmk:citesCase a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:label "cites case"@en-AU .

tmk:citesPassage a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:range tmk:Passage ;
    rdfs:label "cites passage"@en-AU ;
    rdfs:comment "A Manual-internal cross reference. Upstream drops targets it cannot resolve rather than recording them, so absence here is not evidence the source made no reference (Q-08)."@en-AU .

tmk:caseCitation a owl:DatatypeProperty , owl:FunctionalProperty ;
    rdfs:domain tmk:Decision ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "case citation"@en-AU .
